<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    Merchandising.Api - authentication, authorization, business rules,
    database access, transactions, reporting, audit, and API health.
    Spec section 7. Hosting model: spec section 6.4 (Kestrel, then a Windows
    Service at P1-16).

    ====================================================================
    RUNG A. HAND AUTHORED ON THE ASP.NET CORE WEB SDK. See ADR-001.
    ====================================================================

    There is no Visual Basic ASP.NET Core project template, and an ASP.NET
    Core maintainer has stated none is planned (spec section 6.2, reference
    [2]). Every line of this file was written by hand. Nothing here was
    generated from the C# Web API template and then edited, because spec
    section 6.2 explicitly refuses to accept that as sufficient proof:
    "the manual project structure, package references, startup code,
    controller/endpoint code, build, publish, and hosting behavior are
    explicit proof-of-concept items."

    Converted from the plain Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk" shell at P1-02, which
    is the task that owns this conversion and its evidence. P1-01 left the
    shell deliberately unconverted so the change could be observed here.

    ==================================================================
    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THIS FILE, AND WHY
    ==================================================================

    NO EnableRequestDelegateGenerator, PublishAot OR PublishTrimmed.
    All three are set in Directory.Build.props and are deliberately NOT
    repeated here. P1-02 has to prove they are INHERITED; restating them
    locally would prove only that this file is capable of setting them.
    Verified at P1-02 by evaluating the property against this project
    rather than by reading the props file. See
    evidence/phase-1/p1-02-project-file.txt.

    NO PackageReference. ASP.NET Core arrives through the Web SDK's
    implicit framework reference, so the API needs no NuGet package at
    all at this stage. Anything added later must have its version pinned
    in docs/adr.md first (CLAUDE.md section 6).

    NO appsettings.json AND NO Properties/launchSettings.json. Runtime
    configuration is read from an ACL protected host location outside the
    binaries and outside the repository (P1-05). Kestrel endpoints and
    HTTPS are P1-09. Until then ASPNETCORE_URLS supplies the address, so
    no development URL is committed to the repository.

    ==================================================================
    TWO THINGS WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU EDIT THIS FILE
    ==================================================================

    A NOTE ON THE SEPARATOR CHARACTER ABOVE. Those rules are equals
    signs, not hyphens, and that is not a style preference. A run of
    hyphens inside an XML comment is illegal, so the first draft of this
    file failed to load with MSB4025 before the compiler ever ran. It is
    the same defect that made Directory.Build.props invalid XML and broke
    every build in the repository at P1-01. Do not use a hyphen rule in
    any project file comment here.

    This project's NAME activates the MerchGuardrails target in
    Directory.Build.targets, which runs scripts/check-no-csharp.ps1 once
    per solution build. It keys on the name, not on the SDK, so it has
    been firing since P1-01 and is unaffected by this conversion.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Merchandising.Api</RootNamespace>
    <TargetFramework>$(MerchNetTfm)</TargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Merchandising.Domain\Merchandising.Domain.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Merchandising.Contracts\Merchandising.Contracts.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Merchandising.Infrastructure\Merchandising.Infrastructure.vbproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
